From: Zaid <283011337+nottzaid@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Instance culling: cells and occlusion

Instances are sorted into 64 m cells and 16 m sub-cells; whole cells
are culled by frustum and distance band before any instance is tested,
each instance's bounding sphere is computed once, and a draw looks up
its uniforms once per program. Cells use 32-bit indices, trimmed to
size, and a Shape re-reads its instances file for a renderable that
lacks them instead of keeping every transform.

Colour views (main, robot cameras) cull instances hidden behind what
they have drawn: first the instances seen last frame, then a max-depth
pyramid of that depth (half resolution, read back) tests the rest by
bounding sphere; drawn ones are retested a quarter per frame. Meshes
under 64 triangles (impostors) draw second, culled a cell at a time. A
view whose culling hides under 10 M triangles draws in one pass until
it draws 10 M more instanced triangles than it did then. Exact: images
and LiDAR returns unchanged. IRONLARK_OCCLUSION=0 turns it off.

Replaces these patches of the numbered series, with what each measured
as it landed (tools/profile_forest.py, GTX 1060 3 GB, i5-4670):
- 0022-instance-cells
- 0046-occlusion-culling: camera frame 130 -> 62 M triangles, 82 ->
  59 ms; forest with sensing 0.49x -> 0.61x real time; forest view 88 ->
  70 ms
- 0047-instance-culling-cost: LiDAR scan CPU 22 -> 14 ms; forest with
  sensing 0.61x -> 0.62x
- 0056-instance-memory: RAM 764 -> 719 MB
- 0066-occlusion-retry: moving view 54.5 -> 48.6 ms, p99 67 -> 55-61 ms
